Try `npm run test:e2e:setup` again. If Google still blocks it, there's a fallback that always works — sign into the app normally in your real Chrome browser, then capture the session state from that tab:

```bash
# With the app running at localhost:5173, sign in via your normal Chrome browser.
# Then run this one-liner to capture the cookies into the Playwright auth file:

mkdir -p tests/e2e/playwright/.auth
node -e "
const { chromium } = require('playwright');
(async () => {
  // Connect to your existing Chrome session (must have remote debugging enabled)
  // Start Chrome with: open -a 'Google Chrome' --args --remote-debugging-port=9222
  const browser = await chromium.connectOverCDP('http://localhost:9222');
  const context = browser.contexts()[0];
  await context.storageState({ path: 'tests/e2e/playwright/.auth/admin.json' });
  console.log('Saved admin session state');
  await browser.close();
})();
" 
```

That approach connects to your already-authenticated browser tab rather than trying to automate the sign-in at all — Google never sees automation. The README should document this as the fallback. Want me to add that path to the README and update the troubleshooting section?

